Q.10  BESIDE A MACHINE THAT CONSTRUCTS ITS OBJECTS
     The reference model is the machine Turing fixed (1936): it has
     a configuration only once it has written that configuration
     down, and it is charged one operation per symbol written, read
     or compared (Cook and Reckhow 1973).  That is the measure every
     classical method in this archive is already charged in.

     This panel does not race two procedures.  It states what each
     side must HAVE, in closed form, and checks the closed forms
     against the instrumented machine.  By Theorem 15.1 a
     configuration resides independently of any act of retrieval, so
     on the relational side there is nothing to write down: the
     envelope is asserted and every address of it stands.  On the
     machine's side an address IS n written symbols, which is why
     having the envelope costs the machine n * 2^n.  The machine's
     alternative to having the space is to search it, and that is
     what the four classical methods are measured doing in Q.2.

        n    m  models |  envelope: n*2^n  answer: n*models |  assertion: n+1+km     km
       10   42      12 |           10,240               120 |                137    126
       14   59      10 |          229,376               140 |                192    177
       18   76       6 |        4,718,592               108 |                247    228
       22   93      13 |       92,274,688               286 |                302    279
       26  110      48 |    1,744,830,464             1,248 |                357    330
       30  127     106 |   32,212,254,720             3,180 |                412    381
       34  144     130 |  584,115,552,256             4,420 |                467    432
       40  170       2 | 43,980,465,111,040                80 |                551    510

      what is wanted                      the machine        Relational Lysis
      ----------------------------------  -----------------  -----------------------
      the envelope, all 2^n addresses     n * 2^n symbols    n + 1 acts, all resident
      one address                         n symbols written  a path of length n
      what one clause excludes            k * 2^k reads      0 acts, k symbols, forced
      the whole constraint, held          k m symbols        k m symbols
      membership of a given address       k m comparisons    k m comparisons
      the answer, held                    n per address      n + 1 + k m, no term in it

    PASS  the machine's writes are exactly n*2^n to have the envelope plus n for each address it keeps   3/3   [closed form checked against the instrumented machine]
    PASS  the machine's reads lie between one per address and km per address   3/3
    PASS  to have the envelope the machine must write n*2^n symbols, where the assertion is n + 1 acts   [at n = 40: 43,980,465,111,040 symbols against 41 acts]
    PASS  the two agree on membership, which is the one question that is already relational   8/8   [km comparisons on both sides: the panel is not rigged]
    PASS  what one clause excludes costs the machine k*2^k reads to determine and the assertion no act at all   [Axiom 2.2 forces the opposite; Axiom 2.3 places it component-wise]
    PASS  what the machine holds follows the model count and what the assertion holds does not   [machine [120, 140, 108, 286, 1248, 3180, 4420, 80] against assertion [137, 192, 247, 302, 357, 412, 467, 551]]

